      ⍝ Regression (Blake McBride, LanguageVariances.md #39, 2026-08-10):
      ⍝ float display used a hand-rolled digit-extraction algorithm
      ⍝ (repeated ×/÷ by powers of 10) that accumulated several ULP of
      ⍝ rounding error for extreme exponents, and a BIG_FLOAT threshold
      ⍝ (1.7976e308, only 5 significant digits) far below the true
      ⍝ finite double range, wrongly showing large-but-finite values as
      ⍝ ∞. Replaced with libc's own (C99/IEEE-754 correctly-rounded) %e
      ⍝ conversion. Expected values below independently verified against
      ⍝ Python's exact Decimal arithmetic, not just "what GNU APL used to
      ⍝ print" -- e.g. 1E200's correctly-rounded 17-significant-digit
      ⍝ value genuinely is 9.9999999999999997E199, not a "clean" 1E200.
      ⍝
